+# Stage 361 — Five Styles v22: native Square arbitration and Fire header handoff
+
+## Evidence from the current run
+
+The supplied current log identifies s360/v21. It records a ready standalone
+Wisdom dodge profile and installed native Roll hook. Five Single B Quick Runs
+start. All eight logged Dual B Roll entries instead follow a pending Guard
+spin: queued=true, owned=true, data=true; request_roll correctly declines, and
+the legacy host roll handler explicitly hands control to native Dodge Roll.
+The same run resolves Single B's actual Guard row to motion D4; the user
+confirms that Guard now works. These are live v21 observations, not v22 proof.
+
+The log contains 37 cast samples and 17 initial Fire sequence plans. Several
+repeated casts begin directly at motion 3D. Native spell work creation is also
+logged for those new requests, so treating every 3D observation as an automatic
+recovery would suppress legitimate repeat casts. Preserve the native command
+observer and each genuine new request's cast/effect identity.
+
+## Use the prepared native direction for Guard versus Roll
+
+The old selector read dir+0xC at the start of player control. Private inspection
+of the supplied Steam executable shows control calls its direction builder at
+2A6255, then passes that vector in R8 to the action selector at 2A6295. Its
+selector (2A7B30; already documented Epic logical 2A59A0) compares stats+8
+with that third argument's +0xC before selecting Guard or the native Roll start.
+Thus pre-control may inspect the previous vector. This explains a plausible
+source of the misclassification; the logged pending-Guard/legacy-Roll collision
+itself is certain. No fresh vector values were logged by v21.
+
+Move the existing guarded selector to that native action-selector entry. It
+keeps native command priority, abilities, state, deep-water, scene and shortcut
+gates, consumes only an admitted imported Square action, and runs the original
+selector exactly once. Other styles/actors and failed admission stay native.
+Native moving Roll selection and the existing committed Roll hook queue the
+Wisdom Quick Run normally. There is no early imported Roll request. A committed
+Guard, Roll or finisher also blocks the older host Roll/Glide fallback, so a
+changed stick cannot reinterpret its already owned press. The original native
+control pulse, finite host action timing and saved ability state stay intact.
+
+The five-byte selector hook copies one whole position-independent instruction.
+The Steam entry, caller, argument, threshold comparison and native Roll tail
+are checked against the supplied executable. The existing documented Epic
+logical address is added to the paired map; runtime prologue mismatch leaves
+native control intact. No Epic game execution or new Epic binary match is
+claimed. Single B's real Guard row observer remains unchanged.
+
+## Fire recovery follows header contents, not allocation identity
+
+v21 required a different sampled pointer before accepting Fire's second native
+phase, despite separately validating its request and full header descriptor.
+That pointer condition is unnecessary for correctness and rejects a reused
+header buffer. The recording's native Fire flash is confirmed; the log did not
+record pointers or retirement reasons, so buffer reuse is a code-level failure
+case addressed here, not a proven live pointer trace.
+
+Make matching request/header an explicit cast-clock input. Only a sampled Fire
+36/83 may advance to its authored 3D/8A recovery, and only when the current
+sampled header matches that requested recovery. Both unchanged and changed
+pointers work. An old startup header cannot advance the clock even if its
+request has already changed. Both phases still use loaded durations/rates and
+one monotonic donor timeline/cast ID. Other phases, repeated clock reversals,
+new bindings, scenes and action ownership retain their existing retirement
+rules and bounded old-header tail. New actual casts still start new clocks;
+no spell, effect, HP, MP, movement or native recovery state is written here.
+
+Add capped request, phase-handoff, recovery and retirement notes. If the flash
+persists, the next run identifies the actual exit condition instead of inferring
+it from a new cast's first sample. Bound expensive attack-row report formatting
+and IO to once per actor/style generation. All row/data reads needed for live
+contact selection remain fresh; no gameplay result is cached. In v21 those two
+diagnostic lines alone occurred 3,666 times each and dominated the log.
+
+## Validation and remaining live check
+
+443 portable tests pass across 27 suites, including 142 style tests. New tests
+replay reused/changed Fire buffers, old-header publication lag, both phase
+lengths/rates, real repeated Fire recovery starts and the pending-Guard legacy
+Roll collision. Existing host Quick Run hold/release/completion, first-frame
+motion flag, native ability priority, real Guard witness and all five style
+regressions remain. The Windows x64 release, PE interface, 10 new selector
+anchors and 63 source-scope checks pass. These are offline checks.
+
+No fresh v22 game/GPU execution was performed. Verify both B Fire endings,
+repeated and aerial casts, stationary Dual B Guard spin, moving Dual B Wisdom
+Quick Run (tap, hold, release and re-entry), Single B Guard/Quick Run, native
+attack abilities, and style/map changes in a fresh process. The s361 header
+and prepared-Square hook status distinguish this build in the next log.
+
+Keep all five exclusive 0-AP styles, Dual-only duplicate of the equipped
+weapon, native stats/party, Single B .75 Run, A finisher Superglide, Anti's
+weaponless movement/restrictions/aura and existing KH2 spell effects/filtering.
+Prior stage notes below are historical and superseded only where stated.
+
