+# Stage 362 — Five Styles v23: committed Guard and consumed Fire recovery
+
+## Current log and scope
+
+The supplied kh1-dual-wield(2).log identifies s361/v22. Its eight Dual B Square
+attempts become Guard spins, with zero Dual B native Roll-hook entries; the
+same run starts Single B Quick Run five times. The v22 selector-entry override
+still preempts the native choice. Blocking its later legacy fallback exposed
+this as no dodge instead of the previous fallback Roll. This is a v22 regression.
+
+Eight Fire sequences enter recovery, then immediately log CAST END at motion
+3D / clock 0 / queued 0 despite a matching header. The first recovery donor
+frame is 48.36 for Final and 42 for Wisdom: the old startup clock was incorrectly
+sampled as recovery. This is direct live evidence; v22's header-buffer repair
+alone did not fix it. The new log's bounded diagnostics identify the missing
+native queue/clock ordering without the older multi-megabyte table flood.
+
+The user asks whether KH2 Fire's multi-stage combo could explain the flash.
+The existing KH2 controller supports ordinary versus finisher spell slots,
+but this isolated native-cast overlay always selects ground Fire 56 / air 58.
+It does not run that spell-combo controller or advance to a finisher clip.
+Full KH2 spell-stage chaining would require its own combo state and donor
+selection. It is not the cause of the observed recovery-clock reset, and is
+not added here. Native KH1 spell acceptance, repeats, costs, levels and damage
+remain authoritative; no new casts or finisher damage are introduced.
+
+## Replace the native committed Guard branch
+
+The original native selector invokes the movement-state callback before its
+Guard-versus-Roll checks. Even a hook at the selector's entry is earlier than
+that callback. Remove all Dual Guard/Quick Run admission from that entry; only
+the existing Anti unarmed override remains there. Dual and single styles run
+the original input selector. Guard is now replaced at the branch selected by
+its own native ability/deep-water/state/tilt logic, after Roll was excluded.
+The native moving Roll tail and existing Roll-start hook are unchanged.
+
+A small original Win64 bridge replaces two complete Guard argument-setup
+instructions at Steam 2A7C51 (selector +121). It calls the scoped Dual Guard
+admission callback using the native actor, preserving the nonvolatile native
+registers and aligned stack with separate shadow space. If admitted, it jumps
+to that branch's original epilogue: no native Guard context, motion, queue,
+voice or blocking flag is created. If declined, it restores R8=direction and
+RDX=the original RIP-resolved context, then resumes the original actor setup
+and Guard call exactly once. Single B/A Guard stays on that original route,
+including the already-confirmed real Guard-row witness and native blocking.
+
+Before patching, verify the complete prefix, native Roll tail destination,
+context constructor, row-start and motion-queue call destinations, Guard flag
+write and full epilogue. The context global is resolved from the original LEA,
+not a guessed address. The context constructor's Epic logical 2A1970 was
+already documented in drive-model.md and limits.rs; add its Steam 2A3B00 pair.
+Paired builds must satisfy every runtime anchor/layout check; mismatch keeps
+native Guard. Steam bytes are checked against the user's supplied executable;
+no Epic game execution is claimed. The earlier selector-entry diagnosis and
+replacement described in Stage 361 are superseded by this actual branch route.
+
+The portable bridge test executes the production machine code under Win64 ABI
+on x86-64 Linux inside the verified native frame layout. Both accepted and
+rejected paths run; it verifies callback count, actor, restored original
+arguments, fallback count and returning through the correct epilogue. This
+checks real stack/argument/branch execution, not just expected byte patterns.
+It does not execute the game or validate its rendering.
+
+## Do not sample the new phase with the old clock
+
+Private inspection explains the recorded Fire sequence precisely. Native
+motion application publishes actor +164 at Steam 29FD44. The model/skeleton
+callback runs at 2A0574, before +16C is written at 2A05D9. Its caller 29FEC3
+returns before the native motion queue is shifted/decremented at 29FEC8..
+29FEFD. A matching new recovery header can therefore be observed with the
+old startup clock while that exact recovery request is still the queue head.
+v22 immediately latched that old clock, then treated the real reset as a
+reversed/repeated cast and retired the KH2 pose.
+
+For an already sampled Fire 36/83 entering its authored 3D/8A recovery, keep
+the previous donor sample while that exact recovery request remains queued.
+Only consumption of that request permits a phase change and fresh recovery
+clock. Repeated model/FX samples of the intermediate state neither advance
+nor restart the spell. The pending handoff has a six-callback deadline;
+invalid clocks, headers, bindings, scenes and lifetime still fail closed.
+Reused/changed header buffers remain supported. First-cast queue admission
+and real new-cast identity are retained, as are later clock-reversal checks.
+Recovery diagnostics now include the consumed queue state and fresh clock.
+
+New replay tests use the actual logged order: startup 27; new recovery id/
+header with old clock 28 and queued recovery; then clock 0 with that request
+consumed. Both ground/air families, changed/reused pointers, repeated model
+passes, full recovery, unchanged cast ID and pending/rebind/header rejection
+are checked. No native motion, effect, MP, damage or movement state is written.
+
+## Validation and next live check
+
+446 tests pass across 27 suites, including 144 style tests and the real Win64
